The vulnerability I found was CVE-2022-35920

This was a vulnerability in the GitHub of Sanic, a python web server that speeds up web development.
This vulnerability affected path traversal, allowing attackers to gain access to confidential files.

This occurs because there is no check that the desired filepath is within the intended directory and doesn't try to access something that it shouldn't. 
For example, a malicious user could put ../ in the URL of a file request. Coupling 4 of these together will return to the system root, where putting something like
/etc/passwd would return all password information.

My fix is to do a little input sanitation to ensure the path is still in the intended directory before it is used.
This involves adding the following lines:
```real_path = os.path.realpath(file_path)```
```base_path = os.path.realpath(file_or_directory)```
```if not real_path.startswith(base_path):```
  ```raise FileNotFoundError```

os.path.realpath() compresses file_path, removing all of the ../ that may be at the start (file_path is the user input)
if the path specified by the user doesn't start with the intended directory, an exception is raised instead of the server fetching the malicious file path.

Sources:
  National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2024, November 21). Cve-2022-35920. NVD. Retrieved May 16, 2026, from https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2022-35920
